/**

@page peano_cell_connectivity The cell connectivity

\tableofcontents

peano4::Spacetree owns the tree of one rank. It cuts the tree along the
space-filling curve into one section per rank and builds the
peano4::CellConnectivity of the cells the rank owns. It runs every regrid.
An application reads the cell connectivity of its rank. It stores its data in
pools that the slots of that cell connectivity index. A phase runs dispatches
over the spans of the cell connectivity.

@image html documentation/peano4/cell_connectivity.svg

The figure shows from left to right:

- the spacetree cut along the curve into the cells of three ranks,
- the cells and faces of one cell connectivity with their slots, the faces a
  neighbour rank stores too and one level jump,
- the slot moves of one regrid.

# Cells, faces and level jumps

- A cell is an unrefined cell of the tree the rank owns. A cell keeps its
  slot across every regrid that keeps the cell.
  peano4::CellConnectivity::getCellLevel(),
  peano4::CellConnectivity::getCellLattice(),
  peano4::CellConnectivity::getCellCentre() and
  peano4::CellConnectivity::getCellWidth() return its level, its integer
  lattice position on that level and the geometry the kernel derives from them.
  peano4::CellConnectivity::getCellFace() returns the slot of the face with
  number `n`. The faces below `DIMENSIONS` are the lower faces along the axes.
  The faces from `DIMENSIONS` on are the upper ones.
- Every regrid numbers the faces afresh.
  peano4::CellConnectivity::getFaceKind() returns peano4::FaceKind::Interior, `Boundary`, `Hanging` (the fine face of
  a level jump), `Coarse` (the coarse face of a level jump) or
  `Intermediate` (a face of a chain of level jumps without a cell on either side).
  peano4::CellConnectivity::getFaceCell() returns the cell on the lower or the
  upper side of the face. It returns -1 for a side without a cell of this rank.
- A peano4::LevelJump names a coarse face, the side of its coarse cell and
  its fine faces in subface order.
- A face that two ranks store appears in the cell connectivity of both ranks, each
  with its own cell on one side. peano4::CellConnectivity::getNeighbours()
  lists the neighbour ranks. peano4::CellConnectivity::getExchangedFaces()
  lists the faces that this rank and a neighbour store in the canonical order
  (level, normal, lattice). Both ranks derive that order without a message.

peano4::Spacetree::getCellAt() returns the cell of a level and lattice
position. An application finds the cell that contains a position with it.

# Spans

The cell connectivity orders every list so that a group an application loops over is
one contiguous span. peano4::Adjacency::Local names a cell, face or level jump
that reads the data of this rank only. peano4::Adjacency::Remote names one
that reads a face half a neighbour rank sends.

- peano4::CellConnectivity::getCells() returns every cell. `getCells(adjacency)`
  returns the local or the remote cells. `getCells(adjacency, level)` returns
  those of one level for an application that steps the levels one at a time.
- peano4::CellConnectivity::getFaces() returns the local or the remote faces of one
  kind, or of a run of kinds in the order `Interior, Boundary, Hanging,
  Coarse, Intermediate`.
- peano4::CellConnectivity::getLevelJumps() returns the local or the remote level
  jumps, or those whose coarse face lies on one level or on a run of levels.

The remote groups follow the local groups in every list. An application runs
the local groups while MPI transfers the halo messages and the remote groups
after peano4::HaloExchange::wait().

# One step

The pseudocode names every call an application needs. `n` runs over the faces
of a cell. `solution` and `halo` are pools with one chunk per cell slot and per
face.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
const peano4::CellConnectivity& cellConnectivity = peano4::Spacetree::getInstance().getCellConnectivity();
solution.reserve(cellConnectivity.getNumberOfSlots());                // one chunk per cell slot
halo.reserve(cellConnectivity.getNumberOfFaces());                    // one chunk per face
haloExchange.rebuild(cellConnectivity);                               // the faces this rank and a neighbour store
for (cell : cellConnectivity.getCells())                writeFaceHalves(solution[cell], halo[cellConnectivity.getCellFace(cell, n)]);
for (jump : cellConnectivity.getLevelJumps(Local))      interpolate(halo[jump.coarseFace], halo[jump.fineFaces]);
haloExchange.post();
for (cell : cellConnectivity.getCells(Local))           update(solution[cell], halo[cellConnectivity.getCellFace(cell, n)]);
haloExchange.wait();
for (jump : cellConnectivity.getLevelJumps(Remote))     interpolate(halo[jump.coarseFace], halo[jump.fineFaces]);
for (cell : cellConnectivity.getCells(Remote))          update(solution[cell], halo[cellConnectivity.getCellFace(cell, n)]);
peano4::Regrid regrid;
if (peano4::Spacetree::getInstance().regrid(requests, regrid)) { migration.migrate(solution, regrid, transfer); }
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# Memory

The host reads the cell connectivity of
peano4::Spacetree::getCellConnectivity(). A kernel copies
peano4::CellConnectivity::onDevice() by value and reads one cell or face
through the getters that take a slot or a face. In a CPU build both are the
same object. Every array exists once in each memory from which code reads it.

Every data type of an application is one tarch::accelerator::ChunkedMemoryPool
per rank. The pool lies in device memory with a GPU backend and in heap memory
otherwise. `pool.getChunk(slot)` returns the data of the entity at that slot.

# The regrid

peano4::Spacetree::regrid() applies a list of peano4::RefinementRequest
records, rebuilds the cell connectivity and fills a peano4::Regrid: the prolongations
(the parent's old slot, the child's new slot and its position), the
restrictions (the parent's new slot and the children's old slots), the sends
and receives per neighbour rank in canonical order, the slots the regrid frees
and the slots it creates. A new slot never reuses a slot that the same regrid
frees. Every dispatch of the transfer therefore reads and writes disjoint slots
of one pool. peano4::MigrationExchange::migrate() moves one pool through the
record. peano4::Spacetree::finishRegrid() closes the regrid after migrate()
moves the last pool.

*/
